/* book: lead: Network topology studies only the shape of a circuit: which nodes each branch joins, not what the branch contains. Drawn this way a circuit is a graph. A spanning tree picked from the graph gives, in a fixed recipe, a complete set of independent KVL equations (one tie-set per chord) and of independent KCL equations (one cut-set per tree branch). The recipe always gives the right number of equation */
/* book Figure 1.8.1: The oriented graph of the bridge circuit: four nodes, six branches. The chosen tree (solid) joins every node to D with no loop; each dashed chord closes one fundamental loop (tie-set), and cutting each tree branch isolates one node (cut-set). */
