/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Replace every element by a line (a branch or edge) between its two nodes (vertices).
   - A tree is a connected set of branches that touches all n nodes and contains no closed path.
   - These hold for a connected graph; a graph in k separate parts has n − k twigs.
   - The incidence matrix has one row per node and one column per branch, with +1 where the branch leaves the node, −1 where it enters, 0 elsewhere.
   - Add one link to the tree and exactly one closed path appears: the link plus the unique tree path between its ends.
   - Write them as a tie-set matrix B (rows: tie-sets; columns: branches; +1 if the branch arrow agrees with the loop direction, −1 if it opposes, 0 if absent).
   - A cut-set is a minimal set of branches whose removal splits a connected graph into exactly two parts; minimal means that putting back any one of them reconnects the graph.
   - Write them as a cut-set matrix Q (+1 if a branch crosses the cut in the twig's direction, −1 if against it).
   - Each row of B and each row of Q share branches with signs that cancel (B Qᵀ = 0), so the b − n + 1 tie-set equations and n − 1 cut-set equations together are b independent equations.
*/
